      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the area of the square, we need to first determine the length of one side of the square. Since the square and the triangle have equal perimeters, we can use the perimeter formula for both shapes to set up an equation.\n\nFor the triangle, the perimeter is the sum of the lengths of its three sides:\n\\[ P_{\\text{triangle}} = 6.1 + 8.2 + 9.7 = 24 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nFor the square, the perimeter is the sum of the lengths of all four sides. Let's denote the side length of the square as \\( s \\). Then the perimeter of the square is:\n\\[ P_{\\text{square}} = 4s \\]\n\nSince the perimeters of the square and the triangle are equal, we can set up the following equation:\n\\[ 4s = 24 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( s \\):\n\\[ s = \\frac{24}{4} = 6 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nNow that we know the side length of the square is 6 cm, we can calculate the area of the square using the formula for the area of a square:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = s^2 = 6^2 = 36 \\text{ square centimeters} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this calculation using Python code.\n```python\n# Given side lengths of the triangle\r\nside1 = 6.1\r\nside2 = 8.2\r\nside3 = 9.7\r\n\r\n# Calculate the perimeter of the triangle\r\nperimeter_triangle = side1 + side2 + side3\r\n\r\n# Calculate the side length of the square\r\nside_square = perimeter_triangle / 4\r\n\r\n# Calculate the area of the square\r\narea_square = side_square ** 2\r\nprint(area_square)\n```\n```output\n36.0\n```\nThe area of the square is \\(\\boxed{36}\\) square centimeters.",
